excerpt: Great barbers still have quiet gaps in the day. Usually it's not the cut — it's that new clients can't find you online. Here's the fix, in plain English.
---BODY---
<p>You can fade, taper, and line up with your eyes closed. That was never the problem. The problem is the twenty-five-year-old who just moved to Baton Rouge, doesn't know your shop exists, and is about to pick whoever shows up first on their phone.</p>
<p>New clients don't find a barber by driving past anymore. They find you through a screen — and if you're not there, the chair sits empty while someone across town stays booked.</p>
<h2>How people actually find a barber now</h2>
<p>When someone searches "barber near me" or asks an AI assistant for a good shop, the results aren't random. Google says its local results come down to three factors — <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/7091" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">relevance, distance, and prominence</a>. Distance you can't change. Relevance and prominence you can — that's the whole game.</p>
<p>"Prominence" is Google's word for how well-known and trusted your shop looks online: your reviews, your web presence, how complete and active your profile is. Living only on Booksy or Instagram leaves most of that on the table.</p>
<h2>Three things that actually move the needle</h2>
<h3>1. A complete Google Business Profile — with real reviews</h3>
<p>This is the fastest, cheapest lever, and most shops half-do it. A fully filled-out profile plus a steady trickle of honest reviews is what wins the map when someone searches nearby. It matters because people trust it: <a href="https://www.brightlocal.com/research/local-consumer-review-survey/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BrightLocal's 2026 Local Consumer Review Survey</a> found that 97% of consumers read reviews for local businesses. Get this right first — it's covered in how we help <a href="../search-ai.html">get you found</a>.</p>
<h3>2. A website you actually own</h3>
<p>Booksy and Instagram are great for booking, but you're renting your visibility on them — the platform owns the audience, the ranking, and the rules. A simple website that's <em>yours</em> builds lasting search presence, feeds your Google profile, and can't be throttled or changed by someone else's app. You want both: the booking tool <em>and</em> a <a href="../websites.html">site you own</a>.</p>
<h3>3. Show up when people ask AI</h3>
<p>More people now ask ChatGPT or Google's AI "who's a good barber in Baton Rouge?" instead of scrolling. Those assistants pull from clear, well-structured web content and trusted local signals. Structure your site so an assistant can quote it, and you can be the shop it recommends — that's the "AI" half of getting <a href="../search-ai.html">found</a>.</p>
<h2>Where to start</h2>
<p>Start where it's cheapest and fastest: claim and complete your Google Business Profile, then ask every happy client for a review. That alone changes how often you show up. The website and the deeper search work compound on top of it over the following months. We never promise a magic ranking — we promise the work and an honest look at what it's doing. If you'd rather someone just run it for you, that's exactly what we do for <a href="../for-barbershops.html">barbershops</a>.</p>
---FAQ---
SUMMARY: The fastest way for a Baton Rouge barbershop to get found is a complete Google Business Profile with real reviews, backed by a website you actually own — not just a Booksy or Instagram page.
Q: Do I need a website if I'm already on Booksy or Instagram?
A: Those are great for booking, but you're renting your visibility — the platform owns the audience and the ranking. A website you own builds lasting search presence, feeds your Google Business Profile, and can't be throttled by someone else's app. Ideally you have both: the booking tool and a <a href="../websites.html">site that's yours</a>.
Q: How do I get more Google reviews for my barbershop?
A: Ask every happy client, every time, with a one-tap link or a QR code at the chair. Most people are glad to help — they just need to be asked and have it made easy. Never buy or fake reviews; Google penalizes it and it breaks the trust you're building.
Q: How much does a barbershop website cost?
A: Industry-wide, a designer-built barbershop site usually runs $1,500 to $5,000. Our Professional Website is $1,200 one-time — or free when you're on an annual CRM plan, with a small $29/mo care fee to keep it updated.
Q: Will my shop show up when someone asks ChatGPT or Google AI for a barber?
A: It can. AI assistants pull from clear, well-structured web content and trusted local signals like your Google Business Profile and reviews. We structure your site and answers so assistants can quote you — but no one can honestly guarantee an AI mention.
Q: How long until this works?
A: A complete profile and steady reviews can start changing how often you show up within a few weeks. Higher search rankings take longer — often a few months — and we never promise a specific position. What we promise is the work and honest reporting on what it's doing.
